hypotheses.statement:
The function in <a href="#fourier:cardinal" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fourier:cardinal">[fourier:cardinal]</a> extends to an entire function with the representation $$\label{fourier:measure}
 p(s)=\int_{-1}^{1}e^{i\pi ts}\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm d\mu(t),\qquad s\in\mathbb C,$$ where $\mu$ is a finite complex measure, supported in $[-1,1]$, with $\mu(-E)=\overline{\mu(E)}$ for Borel sets $E$. Its total variation satisfies $$\label{fourier:variation}
 \left\lVert\mu\right\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \le25\left\lvert C\right\rvert+\frac{65\pi^2}{18}\sum_n\left\lvert c_n\right\rvert
                 +\frac{32\pi}{3}\sum_n\left\lvert d_n\right\rvert.$$ The atoms of $\mu$ have mass $CP_j$ at $t_j$, for $-6\le j\le6$. For real-axis evaluations, the remaining measure can be folded onto $[0,1]$: on $t_{j-1}<t<t_j$, its folded density is $\sum_n(c_nu_n(t)+d_nv_n(t))$, where $$\label{fourier:folded}
 \begin{split}
 u_n(t)&=-2\pi^2e^{-i\pi tn}
              \sum_{l=j}^{6}(t_l-t)P_l e^{i\pi t_ln},\\
 v_n(t)&=2i\pi e^{-i\pi tn}
              \sum_{l=j}^{6}P_l e^{i\pi t_ln}.
 \end{split}$$ The folded atoms have masses $CP_0$ at $0$ and $2CP_j$ at $t_j$, $1\le j\le6$. Integrate against this folded measure and take the real part to obtain $p(s)$ for real $s$, or any of its real-axis derivatives.

For each $\eta\in\{1,-1\}$, the matrix $I-\eta D$ is invertible, and $$\label{interpolation:finite-matrices}
 \left\lVert(I-\eta D)^{-1}\right\rVert<32,\qquad
 \left\lVert(I-\eta D)^{-1}S_{J,E}\right\rVert<3.7,\qquad
 \left\lVert S_{E\cap[7,16],J}\right\rVert<.09.$$ For $i=1,2$ and $n\in I_f$, the table satisfies $$\label{interpolation:finite-residuals}
 \begin{split}
 \left\lvert\widetilde c_{i,n}+\frac{\widetilde K_{3-i}(n)}{Q_n}\right\rvert
 &<10^{-9},\\
 \left\lvert\widetilde d_{i,n}+\mathbf 1_{\{i=1,\ n=1\}}
       +\frac{\widetilde K_{3-i}'(n)-D_n\widetilde K_{3-i}(n)}{Q_n}\right\rvert
 &<10^{-9}.
 \end{split}$$ For each $i$, the norm of the tabulated list, including node zero, is less than $2.29$, and its norm on nodes greater than $40$ is less than $.00002$.

The exact pair satisfies the absolutely convergent identity $$\label{normalization:curvature-identity}
 \sum_{n\in\mathcal N}r_\Lambda(n)n^2
       \bigl(G_1''(n)-G_2''(n)\bigr)=2G_2(0).$$ Using the global signs, it follows that $$\label{normalization:curvature-inequality}
 H_1''(1)-H_2''(1)\ge2(1-\pi h)Q_1.$$

