For two real $\ell^1$ coefficient lists, conditions <a href="#fourier:interpolation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fourier:interpolation">[fourier:interpolation]</a> are equivalent to $$\label{fourier:coefficient-equations}
 \begin{split}
 c_{i,n}&=-\frac{K_{3-i}(n)}{Q_n},\\
 d_{i,n}&=\frac{D_nK_{3-i}(n)-K_{3-i}'(n)}{Q_n}
          -\mathbf1_{\{i=1,\ n=1\}}
 \qquad(i=1,2,\ n\in\mathcal N).
 \end{split}$$

For each $\eta\in\{1,-1\}$, the matrix $I-\eta D$ is invertible, and $$\label{interpolation:finite-matrices}
 \left\lVert(I-\eta D)^{-1}\right\rVert<32,\qquad
 \left\lVert(I-\eta D)^{-1}S_{J,E}\right\rVert<3.7,\qquad
 \left\lVert S_{E\cap[7,16],J}\right\rVert<.09.$$ For $i=1,2$ and $n\in I_f$, the table satisfies $$\label{interpolation:finite-residuals}
 \begin{split}
 \left\lvert\widetilde c_{i,n}+\frac{\widetilde K_{3-i}(n)}{Q_n}\right\rvert
 &<10^{-9},\\
 \left\lvert\widetilde d_{i,n}+\mathbf 1_{\{i=1,\ n=1\}}
       +\frac{\widetilde K_{3-i}'(n)-D_n\widetilde K_{3-i}(n)}{Q_n}\right\rvert
 &<10^{-9}.
 \end{split}$$ For each $i$, the norm of the tabulated list, including node zero, is less than $2.29$, and its norm on nodes greater than $40$ is less than $.00002$.

For each $\eta\in\{1,-1\}$, $I-\eta S$ has a bounded inverse on $X$. If $r=r_f+r_t$, with $r_f$ supported on $I_f$ and $r_t$ on $\mathcal N\cap(100,\infty)$, then $$\label{interpolation:inverse-bound}
 \left\lVert(I-\eta S)^{-1}r\right\rVert_1
 \le91\left\lVert r_f\right\rVert_1+2540\left\lVert r_t\right\rVert_1.$$

There are unique real lists $x_1,x_2\in X$ satisfying <a href="#fourier:coefficient-equations" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fourier:coefficient-equations">[fourier:coefficient-equations]</a> with the fixed data <a href="#fourier:fixed" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fourier:fixed">[fourier:fixed]</a>. For each $i=1,2$, $$\label{interpolation:coefficient-error}
 \sum_{n\in\mathcal N}
 \bigl(\left\lvert c_{i,n}-\widetilde c_{i,n}\right\rvert
       +\left\lvert d_{i,n}-\widetilde d_{i,n}\right\rvert\bigr)<.00003.$$ The norm of each exact list, including its node-zero coefficients, is less than $2.3$. The corresponding functions satisfy all the interpolation conditions <a href="#fourier:interpolation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fourier:interpolation">[fourier:interpolation]</a>.

The exact pair satisfies the absolutely convergent identity $$\label{normalization:curvature-identity}
 \sum_{n\in\mathcal N}r_\Lambda(n)n^2
       \bigl(G_1''(n)-G_2''(n)\bigr)=2G_2(0).$$ Using the global signs, it follows that $$\label{normalization:curvature-inequality}
 H_1''(1)-H_2''(1)\ge2(1-\pi h)Q_1.$$

Suppose $F(c_j+x)=\sum_{k\ge0}A_kx^k$ is analytic on a neighborhood of $|x|\le R$ and $|F|\le M$ there. Let $D\ge0$ be an integer. For $0\le k\le D$, suppose $\widehat A_k\in\mathbb Q(i)$ and $e_k\in\mathbb Q_{\ge0}$ satisfy $|A_k-\widehat A_k|\le e_k$. Then $$\begin{aligned}
\label{ind:taylor-average-error}
 \left|6\int_{t_{j-1}}^{t_j}F(t)\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dt-
       \sum_{2r\le D}\widehat A_{2r}\frac{a^{2r}}{2r+1}\right|
 \le M2^{-D}+\sum_{2r\le D}e_{2r}\frac{a^{2r}}{2r+1}.
\end{aligned}$$ If the final sum of radii is at most $\delta$ for each of the six pieces, their combined integral has error at most $M2^{-D}+\delta$. Discrete atoms are not included in this estimate and are to be enclosed separately.

Let $p=PR$ have a real $\ell^1$ coefficient list as in <a href="#fourier:list-norm" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="fourier:list-norm">[fourier:list-norm]</a>. Define $$\label{ind:node-P-coefficients}
 A_k(m)=\frac{P^{(k)}(m)}{k!}
 =\frac1{k!}\sum_{v=-6}^6P_v(i\pi t_v)^k e^{i\pi t_vm}.$$ Then $A_0(m)=A_1(m)=0$. The regular series has coefficients $$\label{ind:node-regular-coefficients}
 r_j(m)=C\mathbf1_{\{j=0\}}+
 (-1)^j\sum_{\substack{n\in\mathcal N_0\\n\ne m}}
 \left(\frac{(j+1)c_n}{(m-n)^{j+2}}
       +\frac{d_n}{(m-n)^{j+1}}\right),$$ and converges for $|u|<\rho_m$. For $0<|u|<\rho_m$, the Laurent expansion is $$R(m+u)=\frac{c_m}{u^2}+\frac{d_m}{u}
       +\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}r_j(m)u^j.$$ For every integer $\ell\ge0$ the direct derivative is therefore $$\label{ind:node-derivatives}
 \frac{p^{(\ell)}(m)}{\ell!}
 =c_mA_{\ell+2}(m)+d_mA_{\ell+1}(m)
       +\sum_{k=2}^{\ell}A_k(m)r_{\ell-k}(m),$$ where the last sum is empty for $\ell=0,1$.
